azgor wrote:
> Rodney ideas[2]: Placing a lit candle|lamp inside a greased bag should
> incinerate the bag, any flamable contents, and it should damage you and
> abuse wisdom.

Not bad, although if it's inside the bag, won't it need oxygen to
combust?  (Or remain lit for that matter?)  And I thought the grease
was only on the outside of the bag?  If it was on the inside, then
you'd expect weapons and armor contained within the bag to become
greased too.


> Rodney ideas[3]: Tallow candles should be edible and fairly nutritious.

Not bad, but this falls under the category of random whimsy more than
game feature.


> Rodney ideas[4]: If you are turned to stone, there should be a small
> but finite chance that (after the passage of many years) you will be
> stone-to-fleshed by an adventurer (eg. a human Valkyrie).  That
> adventurer will probably be hostile or might demand payment of some
> kind or there may be a slim chance that it is peaceful.

This one's really nice, although it might be a bear to implement.  The
other monsters on the level would mostly have to be discarded and new
ones generated.

Further, adventurers don't ordinarily use stone to flesh on statues,
instead they smash them open to get the stuff inside, so this would
have to subtly change what occurs when a (living) player uses stone to
flesh on a human statue.  Maybe doing such could earn the player an
ally (a minion-type, not a pet), or allow the player to demand money
from the revived adventurer?  (These rules would apply if a player
found a petrified former player on a bones level.)

The odds of a petrified player getting revived instead of the game
ending would have to be very slim: maybe one in twenty.


> Rodney ideas[5]: Lit candles should go out when you stand next to a
> hidden door, trapdoor or hole. "A sudden draft extinguishes your
> candle."

This is POTENTIALLY a cool idea, although more a whimsy than anything
else.  It would be nice to give players a chance of detecting
undiscovered traps through means other than searching, although candles
burn out so quickly, and are so important later on, that it wouldn't
really be that useful.


> Rodney ideas[6]:  Quaffing fruit juice should reduce hallucination
> timeout - as per urban legend (see
> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drug_urban_legends).

This is a good idea.  It should be a moderate length of time, maybe
half the typical duration of hallucination caused by potion.  Should
potions of See Invisible, which seem to be made from fruit juice, have
a similar effect?


> Rodney ideas[7]: The traditional Catalan/Andorran hat called the
> Barretina was worn by smugglers.  It is sack-like in shape and made it
> easy to carry concealed items.  It would be neat if one could put items
> in such a hat and have a chance that a shopkeeper wouldn't notice you'd
> stolen anything as you walked out the door.

"A chance?"  What if the player removes the item, then puts it back in,
is the chance rerolled?  If it is, then it's too powerful, if it's not,
then there would have to either be some state of the item that is
remembered.  Logically, however, failing such a concealment should
anger the shopkeeper, which would make this riskier than many players
will attempt.


> Rodney ideas[8]: Vlad is known to be weak. Giving Vlad an automatic
> cursed scroll of teleport would encourage using creative solutions to
> defeat him, because otherwise you would have to track him through the
> entire dungeon. Potential options include fire and nymphs (or just
> careful use of a stethoscope).

This is a very good idea, provided that Vlad doesn't just teleport
somewhere else in his tower.  Maybe Vlad should be given a random
object chosen from a set of typical monster annoyances, to make it less
certain what the player should do.  Like the aforementioned cursed
scroll, a worn amulet of life saving, a wand of death, a wand of
lightning (unreflected players may lose rings or wands), a cockatrice
corpse and gloves, or Vorpal Blade if not already generated.

Vlad should probably be given a general enhancement.  My suggestion:
bumped up a few hit dice and armor classes, given somewhat stronger
attacks (maybe from d10 weapon and d10 draining to d20 versions of
both?) and made fast, and maybe a special ability to overcome one point
of magic cancellation with his draining attack.  The random equipment
given to him would supply the rest.  He doesn't need to be made into a
ruthless player killer, just something other than a speedbump on the
road to the Amulet.


> Rodney ideas[9]: If you throw/kick/drop_while_levitating a container
> that contains a !oPoly and that potion breaks, then the container
> should polymorph or some/all of the contents should.

Not a bad idea.  It would be nice to have some other interactions
within containers other than the Bag of Holding pitfalls.  To overcome
this, the player would have to keep potions of polymorph in his
inventory, which isn't too bad once you know about it.


> Rodney ideas[10]: Candy bars should confer blindness to pet types.
> (chocolate is poisonous to dogs, so deal damage to them? - ed.)

Hmm, do monsters, pets included, ever overcome blindness?  I remember
that monsters flashed with a camera never recover....  They should have
an antipathy to chocolate if it's found lying around.


> Rodney ideas[11]: Chastity belts!

Kinda special-purpose, isn't it?  It'd require its own item type too.
Maybe if there were other types of belts in the game....


> Rodney ideas[12]: Swimming while satiated can cause you to drown from
> stomach cramps, as per Urban Legend.

This could become a big accidental killer.  I'm not so thrilled with
this item, some unexpected causes of death are okay, but most deaths
should be a result of bad play strategies, not random gotchas.


> Rodney ideas[13]: Having reflection should act as conflict to, and
> teleport nearby waterlife to you, if you fall in water.

I don't understand the logic behind this, regarding conflict.  Teleport
is a bad idea, maybe if nearby sea monsters were just aggravated....


> Rodney ideas[14]: the moon phases should affect the plane of water

This is not logical (the plane of water is not a terrestrial locale),
but it is interesting enough to warrant consideration anyway.  The
question I have is, what effect would it have?


> Rodney ideas[15]: EARPLUGS!! (Like the blindfold. They help resist
> sound-based attacks, like hissings.)

As far as I can remember, there is only one attack in the game that
could be considered sound-based, the cockatrice hiss, and it's not an
attack so much, I thought, as a warning message.  Deafness might make
an interesting, if not really adverse, affliction.


> Rodney ideas[16]: YANI: Artifact: Rincewind's Boots.  Make you very
> very fast ... but only when running away. (Discworld)

I'm all for more Discworld references, but how would the game determine
running away form danger?  Maybe making moves from a spot adjacent to a
monster to a spot not adjacent to one "free"?

